# above. It didn't before this fix: this branch only set /handoff-pickup and
# exec'd claude, so the interactive session never called `_co_pull_knowledge`
# or `_co_reindividuate` and `.claude/` here carried only whatever agents and
# skills were last hand-placed or checked into git. On 2026-09-22 that gap was
# what left Deck's interactive session on the pre-rename skill set after every
# other crew's `co -r` picked up the rename (issue_01M35K490CAR4Q69B9TAR7Z39X).
#
# GATED ON THE FULL PATH, not the basename match above — the same guard the
# selector and substrate branches use. `--force` clears `.claude/agents` and
# `.claude/skills` before rewriting (preserving git-tracked orphans, but not a
# git-tracked file the profile's plan still calls for by name — see
# convivy-deck#270, which untracks the five deck files that are in-plan so
# this force stops leaving them modified), so running it off a basename would
# let any directory named `convivy-deck` anywhere on disk have those two trees
# cleared. Launching claude in the wrong `convivy-deck` is the pre-existing
# risk this branch already accepts; individuating into one is a new one, and
# it does not have to be accepted to fix the drift.
#
# Gold styling on a pale-yellow ground with brown type (the operator's pick); the iTerm
# DynamicProfile co-deck.json deploys laptop-side (ARCH-078) — the tmux status
# color works from the mini regardless. Gold is a bright accent, so the status
# bar uses brown text (like the commercial/nyh-etc light-accent calls) rather
# than the default white. -r with no prompt resumes the latest handoff. The
# session/alias is `deck`; the dir is convivy-deck.
#
# Shared between `-r` and the automatic staleness path for the same reason as
# substrate's own function — see `_co_refresh_if_stale`'s comment (reviewer,
# PR #835 round 1).
